# SOLID Principles and Function Design

## Purpose
One standard for designing and reviewing classes and functions. The thresholds and severities below are the ones the code-quality, architecture and style reviewers apply. A writer who stays inside them gets no finding, and a reviewer reports nothing inside them.

## When To Apply
Apply when creating or extending classes, services, modules or functions, and when reviewing them.

---

## Thresholds (shared with review-clean-code)

| Signal | Threshold | When a reviewer reports it |
|---|---|---|
| Function body length | over 40 lines | Only when the function also mixes levels of abstraction or does two separable jobs. A long linear table, serializer or test is not a finding. |
| I/O mixed with a transform | any length | One function that reads or writes (file, network, store) and also transforms the data in between. Keep the transform a pure helper the I/O shell calls. |
| Positional parameters | 4 or more | When there is no options object and the parameters are not a natural ordered set the codebase already uses. |
| Boolean flag parameter | any | When the flag selects between two different behaviours, so that call sites read `run(x, true)`. |
| Constructor dependencies | more than 5 | As an SRP signal; name the two responsibilities. |
| Branch chain over a type discriminant | 4 or more branches, extended by the change | When the codebase already uses a lookup map or a strategy for the same kind of dispatch. |
| Duplicated block | 3 or more copies | Reported once, listing every copy. |

Under a threshold, the reviewer stays silent. When the change crosses one, the finding is rated by Review severities below.

## Readability thresholds (shared with review-style)

`review-style` rates these; `review-clean-code` leaves them to it. Generated code, fixtures, migrations and test files are exempt.

| Signal | Threshold | Default |
|---|---|---|
| File length | over 500 lines; over 800 lines | `info`; `minor` |
| Nesting depth (callbacks or `if/else`) | more than 3 levels | `minor` |
| Decision branches in one function | more than 10 | `minor` |
| Boolean condition | more than 3 clauses without grouping | `minor` |
| Unrelated exports from one file | more than 5 | `minor` |

A threshold in either table counts only when the change crosses it.

A stack rule may refine a threshold for its language, in this table form with the row label copied exactly; for that language, writers and reviewers use the stack value.

| Row (exact label above) | Stack value | Marked |
|---|---|---|
| Function body length | the stack's own number | `override` |

---

## S — Single Responsibility
**One class or module, one reason to change.**
- Do not mix fetching, business rules and presentation or persistence in one class or function.
- If you cannot name it in three words without "and", it does too much.
- A constructor assigns. It does not perform I/O or start async work.

## O — Open/Closed
- Adding a variant should not mean editing a growing `if/else` or `switch`. It is a finding only at the Branch chain row: 4 or more branches over a type discriminant, extended by the change, where the codebase already dispatches that kind of thing through a map or a strategy. A chain that merely might grow is not one.

## L — Liskov Substitution
- An override must work wherever the base type is used. An override that throws "not supported", or rejects input the base accepts, breaks callers that hold the base type.

## I — Interface Segregation
- An implementation that leaves methods as stubs or no-ops means the interface is too wide.

## D — Dependency Inversion
- Business logic receives the dependencies it calls into (I/O, network, clock) instead of constructing them. Inside a module, constructing a plain helper or value object is fine.
- An interface belongs at an I/O or substitution boundary (repository, external client, clock). A single internal implementation gets none. Interfaces are named without an `I` prefix.

---

## Iron Laws

**IRON LAW 1: An override never throws where its base type would not.**
**IRON LAW 2: A constructor performs no I/O.**
**IRON LAW 3: The thresholds and severities in this file are the standard for both writing and review. Change them here, never in a reviewer.**

---

## Review severities (shared with reviewers)

`review-clean-code` rates SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, duplication, magic literals and comments by this table; `review-style` rates names and the Readability thresholds; `review-architecture` rates DIP. A row counts only when the change crosses it: debt the change did not create is not a finding. Unused imports and variables, and import grouping or order, belong to the linter and are never a finding.

| Signal | A finding when | Default | Not a finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| Any Thresholds row crossed (Function body length, I/O mixed with a transform, Positional parameters, Boolean flag parameter, Constructor dependencies, Branch chain, Duplicated block) | the change crosses the row's threshold under its stated condition | `minor`; `major` only with a named trigger and outcome | under the threshold; a long linear table, serializer or test; a chain that might grow; 2 copies |
| Any Readability row crossed | the change crosses the threshold | the row's Default | generated code, fixtures, migrations, test files |
| Override throws "not supported" or rejects input the base accepts (LSP) | always | `major`; `blocker` when the broken caller is in the diff | an override the base contract documents as optional |
| Constructor does I/O or async work | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect it causes | — |
| Output argument | a function fills a caller-supplied argument instead of returning | `minor` | — |
| Two change drivers in one class or function | both can be named (validation and persistence) | `minor` | — |
| Interface wider than its implementers (ISP) | implementers leave stubs or no-ops | `minor` | — |
| Business logic constructs an I/O, network or clock dependency (DIP) | always | `minor` | a plain helper or value object built inside a module |
| Magic number or repeated string literal | it has business meaning and no named constant | `minor` | `0`, `1`, `100` and other neutral values |
| Name | it needs a comment, is generic (`data`, `temp`), gives one concept two names, or has ambiguous boolean polarity | `minor`; `major` only when it already produced a named wrong outcome at a call site | loop counters; project abbreviations |
| Comment | stale, contradicting, redundant, commented-out code, or compensating for a bad name | `minor` | intent, a domain constraint, a warning, a TODO with an issue |
| Stylistic opinion, or a future issue with no current violation | — | `info` | — |
